# Built-In SFZ Sampler (Sfizz)

## Overview
- The engine ships a first-party SFZ instrument implemented in `Engine/src/audio/devices/sfizz_device.rs`.
- It wraps the `rust-sfizz` crate and is exposed as a built-in device so it follows audio-thread safety rules identical to other native instruments.
- Godot surfaces the device through `Device.create_builtin_sfizz()` and the asset browser; SFZ files are discovered via `SfzAssetProvider` and loaded using the `/load_file` OSC command.

## Device Identity & Factory Registration
- Device ID: `sonara.builtin.sfizz`, category `Instrument`, variant `BuiltIn`.
- Registered in the audio device factory (`AudioCommand::AddDeviceToChannel`); must be reachable with `device_type == "builtin"`.
- The sampler requires only a MIDI input port; no audio inputs are expected because it is a sound source.

## OSC & Command Contract
- Loading an instance mirrors other built-ins via `/channel/{id}/add_device [...] "builtin" ""`.
- SFZ files are supplied after instantiation with `/channel/{id}/device/{position}/load_file [s:sfz_path]`.
  - The engine maps this to `AudioCommand::LoadDeviceFile`; only the sfizz sampler handles the command today.
  - Paths are interpreted on the engine host; Godot should resolve user selections to absolute filesystem paths before sending.
- Engine status updates (e.g., new `PluginInfo`/`PluginScanComplete`) remain unchanged; the sampler does not emit additional OSC messages on load success/failure.

## Loading Model
- `SfizzDevice::load_sfz_async` spawns a background thread so SFZ parsing never blocks the audio thread.
- Loading progress is tracked via `LoadingState` (`Idle`, `Loading`, `Ready(Arc<Mutex<SendSynth>>`, `Failed(String)`).
- Audio thread access follows two-layer `try_lock()`:
  - `loading_state.try_lock()` returns immediately; on `Loading`/`Failed` the device outputs silence.
  - A ready synth still sits behind `Arc<Mutex<SendSynth>>`; failure to grab the inner lock also yields silence rather than blocking.
- Planar ↔ interleaved conversion uses preallocated `left_buffer`/`right_buffer` sized to the device's `max_buffer_size`. Never resize these inside `process_block`.

## MIDI & Audio Handling
- MIDI input uses the same non-blocking lock strategy; note events are dropped if the synth cannot be locked.
- Rendering loops over the sample count, writes planar data via `render_block`, and interleaves into the provided stereo buffer.
- On bypass (`set_enabled(false)`) or inactive state the device outputs silence because it has no audio input to pass through.
- `reset()` calls `sfizz::Synth::all_sound_off()` when the synth is ready to guarantee note kills on transport stop/seek.

## SFZ CC Parameters
- After an SFZ loads, the device merges `synth.cc_labels()` with a host-standard set (Mod Wheel, Volume, Pan, Expression, Sustain) so unlabeled dynamics (often CC1) stay controllable.
- Labeled CCs go on the `"param"` group (P tab); unlabeled host CCs go on `"cc"` (C tab). `param_id` is the CC number (0–127), range 0.0–1.0.
- `set_parameter` sends MIDI HDCC via `try_lock()`; contended locks queue the change for the next block.
- `take_parameters_changed()` is polled from `mixing.rs`. When true, the engine emits `PluginParameterCount` + `PluginParameterInfo` so Godot rebuilds the device panel. No Godot-side special case — same path as CLAP parameter lists.

## Lifecycle Expectations
- `deactivate()` clears the loading state back to `Idle`; callers should re-send a file path when reactivating.
- The sampler survives device restarts because the sfizz synth lives inside `SendSynth` (wrapper providing `unsafe impl Send`); never access the raw synth outside the guarded Mutex.
- Background load failures populate `LoadingState::Failed`; future calls to `load_sfz_async` overwrite the state and retry.

## Dependency Notes
- The build relies on `rust-sfizz` (git dependency) and dynamic `libsndfile` (`libsndfile1-dev`). The engine has no `build.rs`; the `sndfile` link directive lives in the rust-sfizz crate's `sfizz-sys/build.rs`, so linking changes belong in that repo.
- Test coverage lives in `Engine/test_sfizz.sh` (manual OSC smoke) and `Engine/test_kick.sfz` (minimal patch). Treat these as fixtures when verifying changes.
